Q: solve the social golfer problem with topic constraint? A: <p>I assume your scenario expects 5 sessions, enabling each person to attend a table for each topic exactly once.</p>
<p>Under these parameters, your problem is actually quite easily solvable, because unique pairings are not even close to saturated. By this i mean, there are a lot more people to meet for an individual than he will during those 5 sessions.</p>
<p>Each person should meet 5*4=20 out of 99 other people, which means we do not enter the more constrained search space that makes the SGP so hard in the general case.</p>
<p>Modeling your problem and running any simple local search algorithm like simulated annealing or similar will quickly yield a &quot;perfect&quot; schedule with no repeat encounters and where each person sits at a table for each topic exactly once.</p>
<p>This is an example for how to do this with a simple python script:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>import random
import sys
import time

N = 100          # participants
ROUNDS = 5       # also the number of topics each participant must visit
TOPICS = 5
TABLES = 4       # per topic
SIZE = 5         # people per table

TIMEOUT = float(sys.argv[1]) if len(sys.argv) &gt; 1 else 5.0
SEED = int(sys.argv[2]) if len(sys.argv) &gt; 2 else 1
rng = random.Random(SEED)


def topic_of(p, r):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;A fixed topic plan: participant p visits each topic exactly once.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    a = p // 25 + 1          # 1..4, nonzero modulo 5
    b = p // 5 % 5           # 0..4
    return (a * r + b) % 5   # as r=0..4 varies, this hits all 5 topics


def pair_key(a, b):
    return (a, b) if a &lt; b else (b, a)


def penalty(count):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;0 if a pair met once, positive if repeated.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    return count * (count - 1) // 2


def random_schedule():
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    Build a random full schedule matrix.

    Participants are only shuffled among tables of their assigned topic in that
    round, so topic capacities, table sizes, and &quot;one topic each round&quot; are
    always valid.  The local search only has to remove repeated pairs.
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    schedule = [[[[] for _ in range(TABLES)] for _ in range(TOPICS)] for _ in range(ROUNDS)]
    pos = [[None] * ROUNDS for _ in range(N)]  # pos[p][r] = (topic, table, index)

    for r in range(ROUNDS):
        buckets = [[] for _ in range(TOPICS)]
        for p in range(N):
            buckets[topic_of(p, r)].append(p)
        for t in range(TOPICS):
            rng.shuffle(buckets[t])
            for k in range(TABLES):
                group = buckets[t][k * SIZE:(k + 1) * SIZE]
                schedule[r][t][k] = group
                for i, p in enumerate(group):
                    pos[p][r] = (t, k, i)

    return schedule, pos


def pair_counts_and_score(schedule):
    counts = {}
    for r in range(ROUNDS):
        for t in range(TOPICS):
            for k in range(TABLES):
                group = schedule[r][t][k]
                for i, x in enumerate(group):
                    for y in group[i + 1:]:
                        key = pair_key(x, y)
                        counts[key] = counts.get(key, 0) + 1
    score = sum(penalty(c) for c in counts.values())
    return counts, score


def swap_delta(schedule, counts, r, t, k1, i1, k2, i2):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;Score change if we swap two people in the same round/topic.&quot;&quot;&quot;
    a_table = schedule[r][t][k1]
    b_table = schedule[r][t][k2]
    x = a_table[i1]
    y = b_table[i2]

    changes = {}

    def add_change(key, amount):
        changes[key] = changes.get(key, 0) + amount

    # Remove old meetings involving x or y.
    for p in a_table:
        if p != x:
            add_change(pair_key(x, p), -1)
    for p in b_table:
        if p != y:
            add_change(pair_key(y, p), -1)

    # Add new meetings after swapping x and y.
    for p in b_table:
        if p != y:
            add_change(pair_key(x, p), +1)
    for p in a_table:
        if p != x:
            add_change(pair_key(y, p), +1)

    delta = 0
    for key, change in changes.items():
        old = counts.get(key, 0)
        new = old + change
        delta += penalty(new) - penalty(old)
    return delta, changes


def apply_swap(schedule, pos, counts, r, t, k1, i1, k2, i2, changes):
    a_table = schedule[r][t][k1]
    b_table = schedule[r][t][k2]
    x = a_table[i1]
    y = b_table[i2]

    for key, change in changes.items():
        new = counts.get(key, 0) + change
        if new:
            counts[key] = new
        else:
            counts.pop(key, None)

    a_table[i1], b_table[i2] = y, x
    pos[x][r] = (t, k2, i2)
    pos[y][r] = (t, k1, i1)


def conflict_pairs(counts):
    return [key for key, c in counts.items() if c &gt; 1]


def search(timeout=TIMEOUT):
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    Random-restart, conflict-directed hillclimbing.

    Pick a repeated pair, pick one round where they meet, then try swapping one
    of them with someone at another table of the same topic.  Such swaps keep
    all hard constraints valid and usually reduce the repeat-pair score.
    &quot;&quot;&quot;
    deadline = time.monotonic() + timeout
    best_score = None
    restarts = moves = 0

    while time.monotonic() &lt; deadline:
        restarts += 1
        schedule, pos = random_schedule()
        counts, score = pair_counts_and_score(schedule)

        while time.monotonic() &lt; deadline:
            if score == 0:
                return schedule, restarts, moves
            best_score = score if best_score is None else min(best_score, score)

            conflicts = conflict_pairs(counts)
            if not conflicts:
                break

            x, y = rng.choice(conflicts)
            meetings = [(r,) + pos[x][r] for r in range(ROUNDS) if pos[x][r][:2] == pos[y][r][:2]]
            if not meetings:
                continue

            r, t, table, idx = rng.choice(meetings)
            mover = x if rng.random() &lt; 0.5 else y
            t, table, idx = pos[mover][r]

            candidates = []
            for other_table in range(TABLES):
                if other_table == table:
                    continue
                for other_idx in range(SIZE):
                    delta, changes = swap_delta(schedule, counts, r, t, table, idx, other_table, other_idx)
                    candidates.append((delta, changes, other_table, other_idx))
            candidates.sort(key=lambda item: item[0])

            # Greedy hillclimb most of the time.  A rare uphill move keeps this
            # from being completely stuck if the current partition is awkward.
            chosen = None
            for delta, changes, other_table, other_idx in candidates[:5]:
                if delta &lt;= 0 or rng.random() &lt; 0.03:
                    chosen = (delta, changes, other_table, other_idx)
                    break
            if chosen is None:
                delta, changes, other_table, other_idx = candidates[0]
                chosen = (delta, changes, other_table, other_idx)

            delta, changes, other_table, other_idx = chosen
            apply_swap(schedule, pos, counts, r, t, table, idx, other_table, other_idx, changes)
            score += delta
            moves += 1

    raise TimeoutError(f&quot;no perfect schedule within {timeout}s; best repeat-pair score={best_score}&quot;)


def verify(schedule):
    seen = set()
    visited_topics = [[] for _ in range(N)]

    for r in range(ROUNDS):
        round_people = []
        for t in range(TOPICS):
            topic_people = []
            for k in range(TABLES):
                group = schedule[r][t][k]
                assert len(group) == SIZE
                for p in group:
                    assert topic_of(p, r) == t
                    visited_topics[p].append(t)
                round_people += group
                topic_people += group
                for i, x in enumerate(group):
                    for y in group[i + 1:]:
                        key = pair_key(x, y)
                        assert key not in seen, (r + 1, t + 1, k + 1, x + 1, y + 1)
                        seen.add(key)
            assert len(topic_people) == 20 and len(set(topic_people)) == 20
        assert sorted(round_people) == list(range(N))

    assert all(sorted(v) == list(range(TOPICS)) for v in visited_topics)
    assert len(seen) == ROUNDS * TOPICS * TABLES * (SIZE * (SIZE - 1) // 2)


if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    schedule, restarts, moves = search()
    verify(schedule)

    print(f&quot;valid schedule found by full-matrix local search; seed={SEED}, restarts={restarts}, moves={moves}\n&quot;)
    for r, round_schedule in enumerate(schedule, 1):
        print(&quot;ROUND&quot;, r)
        for t in range(TOPICS):
            for k in range(TABLES):
                print(&quot;topic %d table %d:&quot; % (t + 1, k + 1), *(p + 1 for p in round_schedule[t][k]))
        print()
</code></pre>
<p>Notably, this method of solving this problem will not work very well for most similar problems because if the parameters (number of people, number of groups, number of sessions) are chosen in a way which is closer to the limit where &quot;everyone sees everyone without many repeats&quot;, it will quickly hit a local minimum that is far from the globally optimal solution.</p>
<p>There is no simple algorithm that solves this general case. Even some cases with relatively small looking parameters, such as finding a schedule without repeats for 9 tables, 5 people per table and 10 sessions, are open combinatorial problems. In general, this is an NP-hard problem.</p>
<p>In most cases, the best solutions come from combinatorial design theory which enables us to construct solutions that are far better than anything achievable via search alone. The applicable construction methods heavily depend on the exact problem geometry. There are dozens of them, and many require hard-to-find combinatorial starter objects themselves, such as resolvable block designs, mutually orthogonal latin squares, cyclic partite difference matrices and many more.</p>
<p>I have created a web-tool which provides the necessary functionality to model many scenarios and combines SOTA design theory algorithms with local search and allows you to generate such a schedule for a very broad set of input parameters. I created such a setup for your case and generated a solution which you can access and tinker with here: <a href="http://www.groupmixer.app/share#gm=7V1Lk-O4kf4rFTyrO_h-1M1h7-OwB4fX4cNOdChYEqqKborUkFQ_3NG_a-_7yxygVFUgMj8Q2cNdzzpmDvZMiV8KyC-RSGQiqW_Bx6Y7BvfB09Bfzqfmixrejc_1oIJd8EkNY9N3wX20C8aD6uqh6YP7b8FZ9edWBfc_fQsaDT2rYey7d1GwC7r6pIL7QP9rPU1D83CZ1Bjcf_v--F1nNTdYzddZzQ1W83VWc4PVfJ3VwmC1WGe1MFgt1lktDFaLdVYLg9VindXCYLVYZ7UwWC3WWS0MVguPM415qFlntTBYLdZZLQxWi3VWS4PVcp3V0mC1XGe1NFgt11ktDVbLdVZLg9VyndXSYLVcZ7U0WC3XWS0NVkuPs6p5WF1ntTRYLddZrQxWq3VWK4PVap3VymC1Wme1Mlit1lmtDFardVYrg9VqndXKYLVaZ7UyWK3WWa0MViuPHISZhPDJQizSEAyvH3bXDNT4llb6c39uDnfR3b_pv99pcsfmbyq4z16lL5-IV59IVp9I4RPx6jji1XHEq-OIV8eRrI4jWR1H4v0teKTp6jjS1XGk3t-CR5qtjiNbHUe2Oo6MGceHXdBdTvtRjTpLOgb32S449N04DXXTTVdDnr6etcn_SZ1VPf1Ld-gv3aSGYBec6i_7um37z-q4Vy9_H-dU61l1dTt93T9eusM051-D8edLPahj8PbhZ9U8PU_BfaQHevuWP86LbR7oX5qxmX6vpQa7QH_LMIu-nM-3fyNyQvaL26ZTtR7vvDj39dPToJ7q24eH_vTQdPOwXtcus2rpKqWr0l6FH36d04rJtGIyrZhMK_61Tysh00rItEyfYfuIX-m0UjKtlEwrJWylv3a2MjKtjEwrI9PKjGl92AWjmqame9KbbzD27Sc17G8zrS9Tr73c1J_3h747NtPVu327_qnv9v15ak51ux8P_aCC-2m4qO-7FynneqhPWOz8f_ffgqmf6nb_cDk-qWl_urRTc26bm_5OTbdffD4qPZCrd9SOk_8wCV_872VW8csDj0N9U3n4PtGVrPrxsW-P4GP95ZyU168J30e7oOv3zek89J_USXXT_mmoD8p8JLsOtJnUUN_0F4Uv_-wCraS2Ve2-rTsdAKW7QH2ZBnVSe_X42A-TVpHq6odWHYP7x7od1S54qKfD875V3dP0vFBJ0zVTU7f7SZ3O-vsumpVoFzw2nf3X8H0Y7QLjT_tTf3yhZ_-5bj_u1ZeDGscbVSZ6P9WD1sT1gf1ZDQfVzeYd7oJh3uH2_cNf1WFqPqm93gYn9fRVy24nNXT1pLTxjFPdtlr6sT7PD06XrumeFhPWFnVVID_nJAy_f__-Xa--p6cbuO2f9o-D-vmiusPXq7bnz_cv2hmnelIvsvUHx8uVm33dHU1b3gXHZjy39df9VYHj4VkdL-0C-ypUw_YPg6o_HvvPnfnICxg-sFxhr9-tHi5P-0912xy1xpruUz009RxP3Ozg-sTxcjq_farlTOrLdHtoVs5rhP0H9TiPdzbDnz68LNX__M0F_OYCfnMB_7QuYF6Pl6vgb0E9js1Tp031ejK5nsz39jWLayyCzorXo878cayDI0bIXOz-xVLmaukvH