Q: How to cross validate properties in Spring Boot? A: <p>When a Spring-boot applications terminates the startup process, hence terminating the creation of the <strong>application context</strong>, it launches a few events: like <code>ApplicationStartedEvent</code> and <code>ApplicationReadyEvent</code>.
You could implement a listener to one of these events, who's then tasked to perform your validation.</p>
<p>Re-using some of the code that you have posted, a very simple implementation could look as such :</p>
<pre><code>@Component
public class PropertiesValidator implements ApplicationListener&lt;ApplicationStartedEvent&gt; {

    /*... TODO instance here your LOGGER ...*/

    @Override
    public void onApplicationEvent(ApplicationStartedEvent event) {
        final var ctx = event.getApplicationContext();
        final boolean callLegacy = ctx.getEnvironment().getProperty(&quot;employeeEngine.callLegacy&quot;);
        final boolean callNewApi = ctx.getEnvironment().getProperty(&quot;employeeEngine.callNewApi&quot;);
    }

    private boolean crossValidation(final boolean callLegacy, final boolean callNewApi) {
        LOGGER.info(&quot;{callLegacy} is [%s], {callNewApi} is [%s]&quot;.format(callLegacy, callNewApi));
        if (!callLegacy &amp;&amp; !callNewApi) {
            throw new BusinessException(&quot;At least one of {callLegacy} or {callNewApi} should be set&quot;); 
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
